title: >-
  FlashSpec: lossless speculative decoding with a static-cache CUDA-graph
  fast path
authors:
  - family-names: Myet
    given-names: "Min Htet"
    alias: Mattral
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0005-0853-8301"
abstract: >-
  FlashSpec is an open-source speculative-decoding engine for HuggingFace
  causal language models. Its sampling path (Leviathan et al. Algorithm 1)
  keeps the target model's output distribution, and its greedy path
  emits the target's argmax at every position. CI checks both on CPU: a
  chi-squared test on emitted tokens, and a token-for-token comparison
  with generate(do_sample=False). A batch-1 path on a
  static KV cache with CUDA graphs was measured on one model pair
  (Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct verified by Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct), one Tesla
  T4, fp16, batch 1, with compile on: there it was faster than
  HuggingFace generate compiled the same way. Without compile it was
  slower than plain HuggingFace generate on most measured cells. The
  UCB1/Thompson draft selector is experimental: every measured run used
  one drafter. The Triton and assembly kernels are experimental and not
  on the generation critical path.
